# x * c, c a power of two, with IEEE-like results: mul_pow2_2, then the fix-up.
# The fix-up is that of milestone1.md, "IEEE-like results", with r0 = times_inf(t) (exact, so not
# a fused operation) for prod(t, const inf).
kernel:        mul_pow2_2_full
input:         x = x0 x1
input:         c
output:        w = w0 w1
value:         x*c
requires:      canonical x
requires:      power_of_two c
ensures:       extends w core=mul_pow2_2 lead=mul finite_when=abs_le(x*c,-1)
min_precision: 2
source:        mul_pow2_2, then the fix-up of IEEE-like results (Milestone 1 listing)
license:       core: as mul_pow2_2; fix-up: MIT
status:        proved
domain:        extended
ieee_status:   proved
ieee_theorem:  proofs/theories/IeeeFull.v mul_pow2_2_full_correct
entry:         yes
cone_exempt:   the fix-up selects, after an explicit test that z0 is finite (f), between the core's result and the canonical form of t; F1 to F5 of milestone1.md, "IEEE-like results", state what comes out

(z0, z1) = mul_pow2_2(x0, x1, c)
t        = prod(x0, c)
a        = abs(z0)
f        = lt(a, const inf)
r0       = times_inf(t)
n        = eq(r0, r0)
r1       = select(n, 0, r0)
w0       = select(f, z0, r0)
w1       = select(f, z1, r1)
